From 107d02d037abc959a28212a7ea26f91408488291 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: havok2-htwo <havok2-htwo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:14:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] port 53f72571: conversation cache: a conversation parked
 again keeps at most an eighth of each K/V buffer in reserve (was up to 16 MiB
 per buffer, 0.4-0.5 GB per conversation)

Upstream 53f72571 by BlueKingMuch, applied unchanged (conversation_buffer.hpp
segment_capacity() takes what the buffer held before the append and bounds the
reserve to max(64 KiB, held/8), still at most 16 MiB; allocation_peak() passes
the same; two conversation_cache_test cases; DETAILS.md).

Checked on our side before porting: nothing outside ConversationBuffer depends
on the reserve's size.  The SSD tier (conversation_tier.cpp) sizes its write
budget from image.bytes(), which only gets smaller; conversation_file.cpp writes
size() bytes and reads into a fresh buffer (resize from empty: exact payload,
no reserve); the STRATA_SLOT_PARK / STRATA_PARK_ASYNC captures go through
StagedCopy in staging-sized blocks, independent of segment sizes; the capture
admission (conversation_snapshot.cpp) asks allocation_peak(), which now
predicts the smaller reserve (both new tests check bytes() <= peak).

Comments adjusted: conversation_cache.hpp evict_oldest() and the physical-RAM
gate in generate.cpp said every segment is 16 MiB and above glibc's mmap
threshold, so an eviction is back with the kernel at once.  That held only for
the full segments; with this change the append reserve is smaller still.
Upstream #3115: held/8 makes appended segments of a few MiB or less, below the
mmap threshold (production sets MALLOC_MMAP_THRESHOLD_=4 MiB), so they come
from malloc arenas and an evicted entry need not return them to the kernel;
the RAM gate then reads less MemAvailable back than the cache freed.  Not
addressed here; the allocation side is a separate change (E2).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
